\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Temporal Abstraction.}
The options framework \citep{sutton1999options} and model-free action-repetition methods \citep{sharma2017figar, biedenkapp2021temporl, lakshminarayanan2017dynamic} achieve temporal abstraction by training values on real transitions, insulating them from model-induced bias. In world-model RL, the Dreamer family \citep{hafner2020dreamerv1, hafner2021dreamerv2, hafner2023dreamerv3} and transformer-based variants \citep{micheli2023iris, robine2023twm, hafner2025dreamerv4} train policies in imagination at fixed $\dt{=}1$. Several works introduce temporal abstraction via hierarchical representations \citep{gumbsch2024thick, mattes2024hieros}, goal-conditioned jumps \citep{li2025lsimagine}, or multi-scale state-space models \citep{shaj2023mts3}, but none conditions a flat dynamics model on $\dt$ for imagination-based policy training.

\paragraph{TAWM and the MPC--Imagination Contrast.}
TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm} conditions a world model on $\dt$ within TD-MPC2 \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2} and achieves consistent gains. The critical distinction is architectural: TAWM re-plans from real states at every step, so model errors do not accumulate into the critic. We show that identical $\dt$-conditioning fails in DreamerV3's imagination loop, where errors compound through bootstrapping---transforming manageable imprecision into pathological value overestimation.

\paragraph{Value Overestimation.}
Value overestimation is well-studied in model-free \citep{fujimoto2018td3, vanhasselt2016double} and model-based settings \citep{janner2019mbpo, yu2020mopo}. \citet{lu2018nondelusional} identify delusional bias from policy-class constraints; our CIVO is mechanistically distinct, arising from model imprecision amplified by learned temporal conditioning. \citet{tallec2019making} show Q-function degradation with temporal resolution mismatches, underpinning our finding at $\dt > 1$. \citet{frauenknecht2025rollouts} demonstrate rollout-quality dominance in MBRL; we identify the specific $\dt$-embedding-induced reward bias by which rollout divergence corrupts values. Our factorial diagnostic approach follows \citet{engstrom2020implementation} and \citet{andrychowicz2021whatmatters}.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm} shows that conditioning a world model on step size $\dt$ yields consistent gains within TD-MPC2's MPC framework \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2}. Can the same $\dt$-conditioning bring temporal abstraction to imagination-based actor-critic methods like DreamerV3 \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, where policies are trained entirely in imagined rollouts?

We find that it cannot. Every variable-rate configuration we test degrades performance catastrophically: fixed $\dt{=}2$ loses $86\%$ from peak return, fixed $\dt{=}4$ never exceeds $19\%$ of baseline, and a learned Gumbel-Softmax gate collapses to $11\%$. Through a factorial analysis on Walker Walk \citep{tassa2018dmcontrol}, we isolate the \emph{trained} $\dt$-embedding as the causal factor. This embedding enables the RSSM reward head to develop a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic value estimates---a failure mode we term \emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO). A normalized value-to-eval threshold of $\nve > 1.5\times$ achieves 4/4 separating power between trained and untrained embedding configurations, analogous to the delusional bias of \citet{lu2018nondelusional} but arising from model imprecision amplified by learned temporal conditioning.

Three further findings complicate the picture: (1)~the same CIVO trigger produces stochastic downstream outcomes---full collapse, self-correction, and oscillatory degradation---across seeds of identical configurations; (2)~restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding does not prevent collapse, with one of two seeds failing via a qualitatively distinct non-CIVO pathway (evaluation-driven, increasing entropy); and (3)~the contrast with TAWM identifies the feedback architecture---imagination-based bootstrapping versus MPC re-planning---as the structural factor that transforms manageable model imprecision into pathological value dynamics.

